  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places elongated low pressure along the\nMontana/Wyoming border, with high over the Ohio River Valley.\nUpper level analysis places trough mainly to the west of the \nRockies, with ridge stretching from the southern plains into \nthe Midwest. Over our area, south-southwest flow aloft remains \nin places, while southerly to easterly winds have been on the \nincrease ahead of the aforementioned low. \n\nFor tonight, compact low over the Pacific approaches the West\nCoast while the previously mentioned trough swings \nnortheasterly lifting over the Rockies, while the surface low \nlifts into southwest North Dakota. During this period, main \nconcern may be some patchy fog over western into central North \nDakota with the winds going light.\n\nOn Wednesday, surface low bisects across North Dakota through\nthe day before pushing east-northeast. In the meantime,\naforementioned upper trough continues to lift northeast before \nclosing off and becoming nearly a stacked system by early \nevening. Chances for rain wrap in mainly behind the system \nduring the day into the evening, with the greatest chances (60 \npercent or greater) generally remaining north of Interstate 94, \nwith the exception earlier in the event where elevated chances \nwill be along the Montana border. With that said, total rainfall\namounts are expected to be light, with maybe a quarter inch \nover parts of the northwest. Also, with cooler air wrapping in, \na few flakes of snow can not be ruled out over the southwest. \nThe main concern from this will be increasing winds behind the \nlow, especially over southwest into parts of south central North\nDakota. Latest forecast soundings over far southwest North \nDakota indicate the potential remains for ~50kt winds to mix \ndown, with surrounding parts of southwest North Dakota into \nsouth central North Dakota a little lower, but still ~40-45kts \navailable to mix down.\n\nSystem departs with generally quiet weather for Thursday, though\ntemperatures will be cooler, a trend that looks to continue\nthrough the work week into the weekend. As we get into Friday\nand Saturday, latest guidance indicates and upper low dropping\nsouthward generally along the eastern fringes of the Canadian\nRockies while the earlier mentioned Pacific system pushes east\nand wraps into the flow. Guidance has backed off a bit on \nprecipitation probabilities over our area, but with that said \nmany locations should see a little snow with this system given \nthe cooler temperatures profiles, especially as time goes on. \nGreatest chances remain over north central North Dakota. In the \nwake of this system mainly quiet weather expected, with cooler \ntemperatures.",
